    "body": "## Overview\n\nAccept the standard `reasoning_effort` aliases `minimal` and `medium` in the Python and Rust DeepSeek V4.1 renderers. Fixes #62220.\n\n## Claims\n\n- Map `minimal` to the existing low budget of `50`.\n- Map `medium` to the existing high budget of `75`.\n- Preserve all existing named efforts, numeric budgets, precedence rules, and validation behavior.\n- Keep the Python and Rust frontend implementations consistent.\n\n## Validation\n\nFocused Python tests:\n\n```bash\n.venv/bin/python -m pytest tests/tokenizers_/test_deepseek_v41.py -q\n```\n\nResult: **32 passed**.\n\nFocused Rust tests:\n\n```bash\n$HOME/.cargo/bin/cargo test --manifest-path rust/Cargo.toml -p vllm-chat renderer::deepseek_v41 -- --nocapture\n```\n\nResult: **16 passed**.\n\nAll applicable pre-commit hooks passed:\n\n```bash\nPRE_COMMIT_HOME=/tmp/vllm-pre-commit .venv/bin/pre-commit run --from-ref origin/main --to-ref HEAD\n```\n\nNo model or GPU evaluation was run because this change only affects deterministic frontend effort mapping and does not alter model execution or generated output.\n\n## Details\n\nThe DeepSeek V4.1 renderers previously accepted only `low`, `high`, `xhigh`, and `max`, even though clients can send the standard `minimal` and `medium` effort values.\n\nThis change adds model-specific compatibility aliases in both implementations. `minimal` uses the same numeric budget as `low`, while `medium` uses the same numeric budget as `high`. Focused tests cover the aliases at the Python encoding boundary and through both the request-level and template-argument paths in the Rust renderer. Invalid names and out-of-range numeric budgets remain rejected.\n\nNo overlapping open PR was found through searches for `#62220`, DeepSeek V4.1 reasoning-effort aliases, and the affected Python and Rust renderer paths. Related PRs #52739 and #61094 provide optional generic, operator-configured mappings rather than this model-specific zero-configuration behavior.\n\nAI assistance from Codex was used for issue investigation, implementation, testing, and PR preparation. I reviewed and understand all changed lines and validated the behavior with the focused local tests described above.\n\n---\n\n<details>\n<summary> Pull Request Checklist </summary>\n\n- [x] I used vLLM's `/pr-checklist` skill. (Mandatory for agents, optional for humans).\n- [x] AI assistance was used during the creation of this PR.\n\n- [x] **Design Fit:** Minimizes impact on core components, reuses existing functionality, and justifies added complexity.\n- [x] **Testing and Validation:** Validates the change and ensures any added tests are meaningful and reliable, with CI coverage or documented CI resource constraints and validation performed outside CI.\n- [x] **Code Quality and Style:** Keeps code and comments clear and concise, and updates relevant documentation and examples.\n- [x] **Pull Request Contents:** Includes a brief summary and relevant links, supports claims with evidence, explains root causes and implementation trade-offs, and follows the contributing guide.\n</details>\n",
